 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <p>One or more questions for each outcome at the top of the page; Figure 1 and the formation quiz cover outcome 1, Figure 2 outcome 2, Figure 4 outcome 4 and Figure 3 outcome 5.</p>
 <section class="lesson quiz-lesson" data-lesson="picks" aria-labelledby="picks-title">
  <h3 id="picks-title">Quiz: pick the formation</h3>
  <p class="task" data-prompt aria-live="polite"></p>
  <div class="buttons" data-choices></div>
  <p class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
  <div class="buttons"><button type="button" data-new>Next question</button></div>
 </section>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> You are chalk 3 of four in staggered right. Where do you fly?</summary><p>In trail directly behind lead: about 45° astern of chalk 2, on the opposite side to it. Odd-numbered wingmen fly behind lead.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> In combat cruise you have settled at lead’s 6 o’clock. Why move?</summary><p>Extended flight at lead’s 6 o’clock is not recommended; the best position is 45°, where you can best cover lead.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> The brief says close spacing. How far is that, and measured from where?</summary><p>Three to five rotor disks, from the rear edge of the leading aircraft’s disk to the front edge of yours. Chalk 2 sets it and the rest follow.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> Why prefer stepped-up to stepped-down?</summary><p>In stepped-down the trailing aircraft may fly into the wake of the aircraft ahead, have to adjust their positions and need more power. Stepped-up keeps them above it.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> You are chalk 2 and see a gun position ahead before lead does. What is your role?</summary><p>You are now the tactical lead: announce the threat and call the maneuver to engage, suppress or bypass it. Flight lead takes control back once the threat is bypassed or neutralized.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> Who changes the radios and navigates in each aircraft?</summary><p>Normally the pilot not on the controls (P), leaving the P* to fly and avoid hazards.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> You want to swap lead to chalk 2 in flight. How does it start, and what must chalk 2 never do?</summary><p>Only lead starts it, by a prearranged signal that the flight acknowledges from chalk 2 down. Chalk 2 never accelerates past lead to take over. Better still, change lead on the ground.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> On a formation approach you are closing too fast. S-turns?</summary><p>No: avoid S-turns to lose airspeed. Go around if you cannot slow to the right airspeed, especially when heavy.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 5</span> You are chalk 4 in staggered left and lead calls “Lead is IMC, executing breakup procedure, heading 090.” Using the circular’s plan, what do you do?</summary><p>Turn 40° left to 050 and climb to 1,500 ft above lead’s altitude, at standard rate, and hold that for at least 30 seconds. Stay in contact with lead and call ATC in chalk order.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 5</span> On the ground before a radio-silent departure, the aircraft ahead has turned off its anti-collision light and you are trail. What do you do when ready?</summary><p>Leave your light on and tell lead the flight is ready with the code word (or plain language). The flight departs on the code word, the ATC call or the briefed time.</p></details>

